From 64915a9030807e270da331087d6bc1861fd3cc5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <assistant@brenup.dev>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 01:04:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add Show/Hide required words toggle to Short Answer
 activities

Creators can now control whether the required-words hint (the row of
pill badges showing which words a learner must use, turning green as
they type) is visible while a learner is writing a Short Answer
response. Defaults to on (true), so no existing activity or quiz
question changes appearance until a creator touches the new checkbox.

Important distinction: this only controls the learner-facing HINT.
Required words still count toward correctness/grading either way -
turning the hint off makes it a genuine recall/production challenge
(learner has to remember to use the words without being shown a
checklist) rather than removing the requirement itself. This felt
like the right default interpretation of 'show/hide for learners':
the assessment criterion set by the creator shouldn't silently change
just because its visibility does.

Short Answer questions exist in two parallel systems that share the
same learner-facing renderer (QuizPlayer's ShortAnswer component) but
have separate creator editors and storage shapes, so this touches
both:

- Standalone quizzes (quiz_questions.options, flat JSONB): added the
  checkbox to QuizVisualBuilder's question editor, a default in
  app/admin/quizzes/actions.ts, and passed the flag through
  questionToPreviewActivity so the quiz builder's live preview panel
  reflects the toggle too, not just the published quiz.
- In-lesson activities (lesson_slide_activities.activity_data, nested
  {prompt, questions[]}): added the checkbox to
  InLessonActivitiesEditor's per-question editor, a default in
  app/admin/lessons/actions.ts, and updated LessonActivityPanel's
  questionsFromData to carry the flag from the nested storage shape
  into the flat options object the renderer actually reads.

Both editors expose it per-question (not per-activity), matching how
required_words itself is already scoped per-question in both systems.

Does not touch any of Codex's OBE files - verified via diff, zero
changes.

Verified with a full npm run build - compiles and type-checks clean,
same two pre-existing unrelated warnings, zero new ones.
